tags: x86-64-v1

The baseline level of the x86-64 instruction set: the instructions that every x86-64
processor executes. A processor has this level when the CPUID instruction reports all
the features of the formal, and the operating system enables FXSAVE and FXRSTOR. A
software that needs a later level (for example AVX2) needs another concept. The manual
of the maker defines each instruction: the formal names the parts that apply.

=== formal
class=concept
cpuid.0x80000001.edx=bits 11 and 29 are 1 (SYSCALL, 64-bit mode)
cpuid.1.edx=bits 0, 8, 15, 23, 24, 25 and 26 are 1 (x87 floating point, CMPXCHG8B, CMOV, MMX, FXSAVE, SSE, SSE2)
cr4=bit 9 is 1 (the operating system supports FXSAVE and SSE)
manual=the document 0e5751c026e543b2e8ab2eb06099daa1d1e5df47778f7787faab45cdf12fe3a8:a2bfc03a6f3090a3a1e65f38738e79bffa8a814d580cd272371828853b929397: volume 1, chapters 3 to 11 (the execution environment, and the groups of instructions of this list); volume 2, chapters 3 to 6 (each instruction, with the instruction CPUID); volume 3, section 2.5 (the register CR4)
name=x86-64-v1
rule=the software uses no instruction that a feature outside this list gives
variant=v1
